WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:04.203
-Our first guest tonight is the
longest-serving late-night host

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:06.472
in American television history
and the original host of

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:09.074
this program, which premiered
40 years ago tonight.

00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:11.878
Please welcome back to his show
the one, the only

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.413
David Letterman.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.448
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.838
-Wow. Thank you very much.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.874
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:44.243
Very kind.
Very nice. Thank you so much.

00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:45.311
-Thank you so much for
being here, David.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:46.379
-Well, thank you for having me.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.114
Thank you for being
so kind to me.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.549
I loved Amber and Jenny.
I love the band.

00:00:50.549 --> 00:00:54.888
And you're so lucky, because
everybody is excited to see you,

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.556
and you come out
and you tell your jokes

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.557
and they laugh at
your jokes and stuff.

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:00.993
And of I had
that Amber-and-Jenny thing,

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:04.196
I'd still be on the air.

00:01:04.196 --> 00:01:05.331
Somewhere.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.233
-Somewhere.
-What a great idea that is.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.168
-Yeah, their idea.
They came up with it.

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:10.870
-And the music -- lovely.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.071
-Yeah,
they're really good.

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:14.274
We got a great band.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.309
Now, I do want to make sure
we talk about

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:19.145
the past a little bit --
your past.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.479
But the last time
you were here --

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.316
I want to just refer to this --
you brought a tick.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:26.486
-Oh, yes.
-Yeah. And that was the tick.

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:27.987
And you asked us
to test it for Lyme.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:29.188
-Yes. And?

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:33.393
-It turned out it just
had too much to drink.

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.895
-Nothing worse than
a drunken tick, is there?

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.597
-Walked out of here a
couple days later.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.332
Slept it off
and walked right out of here.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.869
-Well, so it's a win-win,
I guess, sure.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:45.137
-So, have you stayed healthy
through these

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:46.205
last couple of years?

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:47.874
-What have you heard?
-Um...

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.909
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.712
-Yes, I have stayed healthy.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.981
And, honest to goodness,

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.283
if it weren't for
your kind invitation,

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.752
I would not have
known that this is the

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.422
40th anniversary of
the beginning of

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:04.289
what you now do as the show.

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:06.959
I would not have known.
-No idea?

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.629
-So thank you again for that.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.664
-What were your memories
leading up to that night,

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:12.732
the premiere?

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:13.833
You had Bill Murray as
your first guest.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.802
-Oh, my God, yes.
Well, two things.

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:18.972
First of all,
what was I consumed by?

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.574
Paralytic fear.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:25.244
-Because we had blown up
the NBC daytime schedule

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.745
a year previously.

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:28.381
I -- We had a show --

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.984
A lot of us had a show that
we thought was just great.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:36.689
And it was on for 90 minutes,
live, like 9:00 to 10:30 on NBC.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.558
And it replaced
two or three game shows,

00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:44.096
and it turned out, America
didn't want them replaced.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.732
Certainly didn't
want them replaced by me.

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:50.870
But when you're young, one of
the nice complementary features

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.304
of being young is being dumb.

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:53.639
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:58.210
-And we all thought, "Oh,
television is the way television

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.047
is because we're not there yet.

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:04.784
When we get to television,
it'll be fine."

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:09.722
We were wrong about that
and many, many other things.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:14.727
So, we were on the air for --
I don't know -- maybe six weeks.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.462
-That's it?
For the morning show.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:21.000
-That's it.
Six weeks, maybe two months.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.234
I don't know.

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:25.504
And then I had to go to
the end of the line.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.106
-And how long did you
wait at the end of the line

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.374
before "Late Night" happened?

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:29.675
Not too long.

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:32.745
-Well, speak for yourself.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.081
It seemed like an eternity.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:38.084
Because in show business,
if you screw something up

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:43.022
like blowing up
a network's daytime schedule --

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:46.693
you're welcome -- you know,
it could be a while

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.760
before they
call your number again.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.794
-Yeah.

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:51.230
-And -- But, eventually,
we came back.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.866
And I was still living with
this trepidation that,

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.435
"Well, this can't possibly
go any better than

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:57.970
the other one went."

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.239
But, so, you have that aside,
and on the other hand,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.874
you have Bill Murray
and you think,

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:04.910
"Oh, my God,
who can't love Bill Murray?"

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.545
So I had mixed emotions.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:11.317
And then, the night of the show,
I just felt fantastic.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:15.087
And then that lasted, I guess,
until my feet hit the floor

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:18.423
in the morning,
and then the paralytic fear

00:04:18.423 --> 00:04:19.591
starts all over again.

00:04:19.591 --> 00:04:22.761
-Yeah, in those early days,
a good show

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:24.263
doesn't last particularly long,

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:25.331
as far as putting
your fear to bed.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:26.599
-Well, that's right.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.333
But I don't know that we were
doing a good show,

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:29.669
Bill Murray excepted,

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:32.238
but we tried to be
as unusual as possible,

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:35.708
because there was nothing else
on TV in those days.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:38.777
It went away right after
Johnny Carson.

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:41.413
So then we had this show,
and we tried to make it

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:45.351
as unusual as we possibly could,
because we knew,

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.519
"How many people
are actually watching?"

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:49.655
And they said, "Alright,
we'll give you six weeks."

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:51.691
So they gave us six weeks,
and at the end of six weeks,

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:54.560
they said, "Okay, you can come
back and do six more weeks."

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.064
So, through the first year,
we were never certain,

00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:01.167
beyond a month and a half or
two months, if we had a future.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:04.937
And then things started to --

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:07.206
You know, the cement started to
harden a little bit.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.175
-Did you have a moment,
then, where you started feeling

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:12.845
relaxed with the show and that
you'd be around for a long time?

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.748
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:21.854
-I'm trying to think if I had
one waiting to come out here.

00:05:22.521 --> 00:05:23.589
I don't know.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:25.157
No, show business is
awful and ugly.

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:26.191
How about you?

00:05:26.191 --> 00:05:29.061
Now you, Mr. Established,
ready to go,

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:32.464
"Here I am, thank you very
much," you're in good shape.

00:05:32.464 --> 00:05:34.266
-Well, I think I obviously
had a different journey.

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:36.268
I was terrified when it started,
but I had just finished up

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:37.803
a very long run at "SNL,"

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:39.572
so I did feel I was
comfortable in the building.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:42.208
I, you know --
-And people liked you.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:43.809
People knew you.
-They certainly knew me.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:44.811
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:05:44.811 --> 00:05:46.579
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:48.981
There was name recognition.

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:52.117
But it took me a while.

00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:53.352
I would say it took me
about 18 months.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:54.753
I will say this.

00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:57.089
I had an interesting journey,
like everybody do in shows

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:58.324
over the last two years,

00:05:58.324 --> 00:05:59.725
of going and
doing the show at home.

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.361
I think you would have --
Terrible reason.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:04.430
I think you would have
enjoyed doing shows at home.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.432
-Yes, you're absolutely right.

00:06:07.432 --> 00:06:09.468
Now, the one thing
that I still would insist on

00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:10.703
at home would be makeup.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.305
-Uh-huh.

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:14.173
-I'm not going to put on
a show without makeup.

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:15.507
-Yeah.

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:19.011
-But the constraints of that,
you don't look at them

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:22.081
as constraints so much
as opportunities.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.984
"Here I am at the house.
What can I do?"

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:28.487
And, you know, I would go wake
my family up one night,

00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:30.289
see how that's going.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.124
And how long were you at home?

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:35.761
-I was at home for almost,
I guess, 9, 10 months.

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:39.899
And it was exhilarating,
terrifying,

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.968
but it was so interesting,
because you have

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.338
this audience here, and I love
having an audience here.

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.674
-Now, while you were at home,
was the audience still here?

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:52.244
-I did insist on it.

00:06:52.244 --> 00:06:55.214
And, looking back,
it was a public-health risk,

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.249
and I apologize for it.

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:59.985
-So the audience isolated for
10 months here at the studio.

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:01.253
-It was the same audience.

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:02.455
We didn't bring in a
different group every night.

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:04.690
-Oh, I see. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.360
You have food and blankets.
You're fine.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:11.196
-But it was --
I felt more connection

00:07:11.196 --> 00:07:12.832
with the audience at home
than I ever had before,

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:15.033
because there was
nobody else out there.

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:17.370
-Right.
And was there an intimacy

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.072
that developed that heretofore
had not existed?

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:23.542
-It was the first time
that I understood a thing

00:07:23.542 --> 00:07:25.377
that I should have understood
from watching your show,

00:07:25.377 --> 00:07:26.579
which is, the people
that watch your show

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:27.880
tend to watch it every night,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.049
and so you can sort of
lay in recurring bits.

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:31.917
You can make
a callback joke to something.

00:07:31.917 --> 00:07:33.452
On Thursday,
you can call back Monday

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:36.655
with the sense that they
probably were there with you.

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:38.524
And that became a nice, intimate
connection that time.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:39.658
-Yeah.

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:40.992
-You mentioned you would
wake up your family.

00:07:40.992 --> 00:07:44.263
One of the things that I first
remember about your show,

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:47.065
your mom became a player
on your show.

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:50.336
How did you first think that
she would be a fun character

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.403
to bring on television?

00:07:51.403 --> 00:07:52.772
-Well, first of all,
this is exciting,

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:54.473
because in her life, in my life,

00:07:54.473 --> 00:07:56.875
no one has ever described
my mom as a player.

00:07:56.875 --> 00:07:59.311
So thank you for that.

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:01.581
That's great!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:08.254
I was driving to work one day,
in the old days,

00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:10.255
and Howard Stern
was talking to his mother.

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:11.590
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:16.295
-And I kind of put the pieces
together and thought,

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.164
"I have a mother."

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:22.100
And it was because of Howard.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.671
Then we started putting
my mother on the show.

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:29.341
And people -- She became so
popular because she,

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:32.077
in many ways for television,
the perfect mother.

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:36.516
Snow-white, beautiful hair,
a lovely woman,

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:39.285
and -- But the thing
about my mom --

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:41.386
And everybody would say,
"Oh, we love your mother.

00:08:41.386 --> 00:08:42.822
We love your mother."

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:46.225
And I would say, "Yeah, try
living with her for 18 years."

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:53.466
But she was the
textbook definition of taciturn.

00:08:53.466 --> 00:08:57.737
-Mm-hmm.
-Just "Mom?" A lot of that.

00:08:59.404 --> 00:09:03.042
So, we decided to send her to
Norway for the Olympics,

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.677
I think after --

00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:06.145
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.145 --> 00:09:07.980
-Not to compete.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.548
-Oh, no, she was competing.
-She did compete.

00:09:09.548 --> 00:09:12.117
-Yeah, no.
-She did poorly, if I recall.

00:09:12.117 --> 00:09:13.819
-She did not medal.

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:15.888
We don't say she did poorly.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:18.391
We say she did not medal. No.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.426
[ Applause ]

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:25.197
So, what I found with mom was,

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:28.600
it was like I was doing
a ventriloquist act,

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.269
because in the question,

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:32.938
I would have to supply her
with the answer.

00:09:32.938 --> 00:09:37.943
And we would get a lot of,
"David?"

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:41.213
And people thrived on this
and loved it,

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:45.184
and I thought it was a great way
to bring us close together.

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:46.485
-Yeah.

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:48.988
-I don't know what your life
was with your family,

00:09:48.988 --> 00:09:54.159
but I felt an estrangement.

00:09:54.159 --> 00:09:56.629
And I thought this would --
And it did.

00:09:56.629 --> 00:09:58.564
We, in fact, became closer.

00:09:58.564 --> 00:10:03.703
But the idea that she was just a
ball of laughs and great fun,

00:10:03.703 --> 00:10:06.538
eh...

00:10:06.538 --> 00:10:09.941
I'd like to see
the paperwork on that.

00:10:09.941 --> 00:10:14.112
But she -- [ Laughs ]

00:10:14.112 --> 00:10:16.148
She -- And God bless her.

00:10:16.148 --> 00:10:18.250
She lived to be almost 94.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:19.752
-Wow.
-Yeah, it was amazing.

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:22.154
[ Applause ]

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:25.524
And as people say sometimes,
"Good for her.

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:29.728
She died doing
what she loved -- sleeping."

00:10:29.728 --> 00:10:31.430
So...
[ Laughter ]

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:44.910
We -- I cleared that
with the family.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.312
-Oh, that's good.
-Yeah.

00:10:46.312 --> 00:10:47.946
-That's very wise of you.
That's very wise.

00:10:47.946 --> 00:10:49.515
-Thank you very much.
-You know the business.

00:10:49.515 --> 00:10:51.483
Now we're going to go to
commercial.

00:10:51.483 --> 00:10:53.319
This is very exciting.
We have a clip.

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:54.820
-Oh, yeah,
now let me intervene.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.622
And I'm sorry.

00:10:56.622 --> 00:11:00.659
This clip -- And people come on
shows with clips and stuff,

00:11:00.659 --> 00:11:02.861
and there's never been a clip
produced and shown

00:11:02.861 --> 00:11:07.566
on a talk show that has impelled
anyone to go see the movie.

00:11:07.566 --> 00:11:10.436
They just -- It's like
60 minutes for, you know,

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:11.470
whatever.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.041
Seth and I can chat about you.

00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:17.976
And this clip is only
representational.

00:11:17.976 --> 00:11:19.611
-Mm-hmm.
-It's not humorous.

00:11:19.611 --> 00:11:20.880
It's not funny.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.384
It's representational of
the nonsense that we pursued

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:28.253
each and every night
when I first started the show,

00:11:28.253 --> 00:11:32.525
which, by the way, is so much
better now, thanks to you.

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:34.760
-Well, I don't know
if I agree with that.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.528
And you can make
your own judgment

00:11:36.528 --> 00:11:39.465
after you watch
this representational clip.

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:40.799 line:0%
-When you work here at NBC,

00:11:40.799 --> 00:11:43.402 line:0%
one of the things you do a lot
is ride in elevators.

00:11:43.402 --> 00:11:45.271 line:0%
And since a lot of sports
originated from

00:11:45.271 --> 00:11:47.272 line:0%
real-life activities,
there is no better sport

00:11:47.272 --> 00:11:50.742 line:0%
for this building than,
of course, elevator races.

00:11:50.742 --> 00:11:52.277 line:0%
You are not allowed
to ask anyone

00:11:52.277 --> 00:11:54.346 line:0%
to hold the elevator
for you.

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:58.117 line:0%
You must ride only on
your assigned elevator.

00:11:58.117 --> 00:12:01.386 line:0%
And you must be polite
to all other passengers.

00:12:01.386 --> 00:12:04.789 line:0%
-David, the atmosphere down
here is incredibly tense.

00:12:04.789 --> 00:12:07.292 line:0%
I'd have to liken it to
the scene at the 18th green

00:12:07.292 --> 00:12:08.960 line:0%
